{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe security gate cannot run on a worker that does not already carry the scanner binaries. `RealSemgrepScanner` and `RealTrivyScanner` in `adapters/real/security_scanner.py` shell out to bare `semgrep` and `trivy`, so on the activity worker image, which ships neither, the gate raises `FileNotFoundError` from the subprocess layer rather than reporting a scanner problem.\n\nRunning the gate against `archive-massgov-pfml` also exposed a second defect. The semgrep adapter passed `--error=false`, and `semgrep --error` takes no argument, so that adapter had never produced a successful scan.\n\n## Approach\n\nRun each scanner from its own published image in a pod, reusing the pod transport the checks already use, and keep the existing subprocess path available for workers that do carry the binaries.\n\nThis fits because the scanners are ordinary container images that the vendors publish, so pinning an image is cheaper and more reproducible than baking two toolchains into the worker image and keeping them updated. Reusing `gates/pod_runner.py` also means the gate inherits the pod lifecycle the checks path already proved out, rather than adding a second execution mechanism.\n\nBecause the scanner runs inside a pod, the paths it reports are pod paths. The findings are remapped back to repository-relative paths before the gate sees them, so the change is invisible to every consumer of `ScanFinding`.\n\nA missing binary on the subprocess path raises `ScannerError` rather than `FileNotFoundError`, so the gate reports a scanner failure instead of an unhandled exception.\n\n### Classifying infrastructure failure\n\nA pod that cannot be created, pulled, or reached is infrastructure failing, not a vulnerability. `SecurityMediator.scan` re-raises `ScannerUnavailableError` so the activity retries the scan, and converts every other exception into a finding, which fails the gate closed. Left unclassified, a transient image pull would be recorded as a security finding and block promotion.\n\n`PodCommandRunner.__call__` therefore catches `PodEngineError` from `create`, `wait_ready`, `copy_in`, and `exec_capture`, and re-raises it as `ScannerUnavailableError`. This matches what `gates/gate_engine.py` already does on its own pod path. A pod that was created is still deleted when the scan fails, because teardown sits in an inner `finally` nested inside the classifying `except`.\n\nReadiness is bounded. `KubectlPodOps` takes `ready_timeout_seconds`, defaulting to 180, and passes it as `--timeout=180s` on the wait. `exec_capture` is not bounded by the pod transport, so a scanner that hangs after starting is bounded by the Temporal activity timeout instead.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `scripts/config.py` gains `scanner_execution`, `semgrep_image`, and `trivy_image`.\n- `services/orchestration/gates/scanner_pod.py` is new and holds `PodCommandRunner`, `pod_command_runner`, `scanner_pod_name`, `remap_paths`, `restore_paths`, and `scan_script`.\n- `services/orchestration/gates/builtin.py` gains `_scanner_backend` to select subprocess or pod execution.\n- `adapters/real/security_scanner.py` raises `ScannerError` when the binary is absent and drops the invalid `--error=false` argument.\n- `docs/gate-engine.md` documents running the scanners from their own images.\n- Tests: `tests/unit/test_scanner_pod.py` is new.\n\n## Grounded assumptions\n\n- The scanners are invoked through a `CommandRunner` seam in `adapters/real/security_scanner.py`, so a pod-backed runner can be substituted without touching the finding parsers.\n- `gates/pod_runner.py` already creates and tears down pods for the checks path, so the scanner path reuses it rather than adding new lifecycle code.\n- `SecurityMediator.scan` re-raises only `ScannerUnavailableError` and turns every other exception into a finding, so that type is the sole way to signal a retryable failure.\n- `trivy fs --format json --quiet <path>` was run against the pinned `docker.io/aquasec/trivy:0.74.0` image. It exits 0, writes a JSON document to stdout, and `--quiet` suppresses the vulnerability-database logs that otherwise reach stderr, so the flag is accepted after the subcommand.\n- Trivy omits `Results` entirely when it finds nothing to scan, and `_trivy_findings` reads `payload.get(\"Results\") or []`, so an empty report parses to zero findings rather than raising.\n- Buildah short-name resolution covers only official Docker Hub images, so a third-party organisation image must be written as `docker.io/<org>/<image>` or it fails with \"short-name resolution enforced but cannot prompt without a TTY\".\n- `kubectl exec` without `-t` keeps stdout and stderr on separate streams, which is what allows `scan_script` to return parseable JSON on stdout.\n\n## Testing\n\nHappy path: a scanner runs in a pod, emits JSON on stdout, and the findings come back with repository-relative paths. Asserted by `tests/unit/test_scanner_pod.py`.\n\nFailure and edge paths: a missing binary on the subprocess path raises `ScannerError` rather than `FileNotFoundError`. A pod transport failure at create, wait-ready, or exec is raised as `ScannerUnavailableError` so the activity retries instead of the gate failing closed. A pod that was created is deleted even when the scan fails. Two scans running at once do not collide on a pod name. A finding whose location text contains the workspace prefix has only its location fields rewritten, not arbitrary body text. The scanner writes its output outside the tree it scans, so it never reports findings against its own output file.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Reported finding paths are repository-relative, verified by `tests/unit/test_scanner_pod.py` asserting the pod workspace prefix is stripped.\n- [ ] Two concurrent semgrep scans use distinct pod names, verified by a unit test asserting two calls to `scanner_pod_name` differ.\n- [ ] A missing binary raises `ScannerError`, verified by a unit test.\n- [ ] A pod transport failure raises `ScannerUnavailableError`, verified by a parametrised unit test covering create, wait-ready, and exec failures.\n- [ ] A pod created before a failing scan is still deleted, verified by a unit test asserting the delete call was made.\n- [ ] `semgrep` is invoked without `--error=false`, verified by `! grep -rq \"error=false\" adapters/` exiting 0.\n- [ ] `ruff check services tests scripts adapters` exits 0.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] With `scanner_execution` set to pod, a semgrep scan runs against `archive-massgov-pfml` without either binary present on the worker, returning findings rather than raising.\n  provenance: environment=local-colima-k3s; principal=joeoakhart; privilege_posture=broader; instrument=live-call — the gate pod runs privileged because buildah cannot unpack a layer unprivileged under k3s\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A trivy scan through the same pod path exits 0 and returns a parseable report.\n  provenance: environment=local-colima-k3s; principal=joeoakhart; privilege_posture=broader; instrument=live-call — the pinned trivy image was also exercised directly through docker to pin the subcommand and flag surface"}},"env_id":"8c3ef925-fbdc-4d88-8d59-f391cf59568c","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789449568986128001,"uuid":"dbdb04d4-348d-460f-bbe4-6b4e69fd2df0"}